# Who has to approve what.
#
# A path listed here needs a review from one of its owners before it can
# merge. A path not listed here needs none, so a front-end fix still goes in
# the moment CI is green. The rule is not seniority -- it is blast radius.
# The things below are the ones where a mistake is expensive, slow to notice,
# or impossible to take back.
#
# The last matching line wins, which is why the two front-end exceptions sit
# at the bottom rather than beside their directories.
#
# One owner, deliberately. Anybody may review and approve a pull request --
# that is worth having and costs nothing -- but on these paths the approval
# that unblocks a merge is the owner's.
#
# The cost of that is worth writing down rather than discovering: GitHub does
# not let anybody approve their own pull request, so the owner cannot approve
# their own change to an owned path. Merging it needs the repository admin
# bypass, which is the owner here too. If that starts feeling like a
# formality, the answer is a second owner rather than a wider bypass.

# --------------------------------------------------------------- the gate ---
# This file and the workflows together. A change here can switch off every
# other line in it, and a workflow holds the OIDC credential that deploys to
# Azure -- so a pull request that edits one is the one most worth reading.
/.github/                           @tobibytes

# ------------------------------------------------------------ the backend ---
# Sessions, permissions, and everybody's personal data. src/atlas also holds
# MorganHacks.Migrations, where anything added under Scripts/ runs against
# every environment, forever, in order -- there is no undo and no second
# chance to review it after the fact.
/src/atlas/                         @tobibytes
/src/harbor/                        @tobibytes
/src/lark/                          @tobibytes

# ---------------------------------------------------------- the platform ---
# Bicep, the deploy scripts, and the libraries every service imports. A
# change to libs/ is a change to all six at once.
/deploy/                            @tobibytes
/libs/                              @tobibytes

# ------------------------------------------- two front-end files, and why ---
# The front ends are otherwise unowned on purpose: a copy change or a layout
# fix should not wait on anybody.
#
# These two are different. next.config.ts holds the rewrite that sends every
# API call -- carrying the session cookie -- to whatever API_ORIGIN names, and
# lib/api.ts is the code that attaches that cookie. Repointing either one is
# how an organizer's session leaves the building, and it looks like an
# ordinary front-end edit in a diff.
/src/portaladmin/next.config.ts     @tobibytes
/src/portalweb/next.config.ts       @tobibytes
/src/portalforms/next.config.ts     @tobibytes
/src/portaladmin/lib/api.ts         @tobibytes
/src/portalweb/lib/api.ts           @tobibytes
/src/portalforms/lib/api.ts         @tobibytes
